1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:06,410
In Upper Arlington, Ohio, the least green part of town was 4.1 degrees hotter than the greenest part.

2
00:00:06,510 --> 00:00:10,510
So where should the city plant new trees?

3
00:00:10,510 --> 00:00:16,140
Satellites take a new picture of every street every few days. That's too much to look through.

4
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:22,250
So we built a way to search satellite photos in plain English, without training a new AI.

5
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:25,850
Type “river corridor”.

6
00:00:25,850 --> 00:00:31,350
All 6 top results really are the river. We checked them against our own labels.

7
00:00:31,350 --> 00:00:37,810
Any words work, like “tall trees near a river”. The number is a match score, not a percent.

8
00:00:37,910 --> 00:00:43,910
The Heat Lab adds two satellite measurements: how green, and how hot.

9
00:00:43,910 --> 00:00:49,910
Greener squares are cooler, by about 1.7 degrees for every step in green.

10
00:00:49,910 --> 00:00:54,910
“Plant first” marks hot squares with few trees.

11
00:00:54,910 --> 00:00:59,290
Search “parking lot”, see the hottest ones, and download the list.

12
00:00:59,390 --> 00:01:05,390
How does it work? One AI turns photos into numbers. Another turns words into numbers.

13
00:01:05,390 --> 00:01:11,390
We line the two up with one rotation. No training.

14
00:01:11,390 --> 00:01:18,990
On new photos, the right land type comes first 96% of the time. A random rotation gets 15%.

15
00:01:19,090 --> 00:01:26,250
A trained ResNet labels photos better: 98% to our 81%. But it can't search by words.

16
00:01:26,350 --> 00:01:33,350
In our own town, it's a tie: 64% right, versus 62% for always guessing “Residential”.

17
00:01:33,350 --> 00:01:39,100
Rivers: zero out of ten right. From space, a thin river looks like a road.

18
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:43,800
Next: sharper photos, and an AI that can say “I don't know”.

19
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:48,800
